    "['interface [interface_name] passive', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ospf 1', 'parent_command': ['ospf 1'], 'root': 'ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nospf 1\\n area 0.0.0.0\\n  network 3.3.3.9 0.0.0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Huawei, OSPF configuration starts with \"ospf [process-id]\". Here we use process ID 1 as it\\'s commonly the default.\\n2. The area is specified in dotted decimal format (0.0.0.0 for area 0)\\n3. Instead of marking an interface as passive, Huawei uses the \"network\" command to advertise the loopback address directly\\n4. The 0.0.0.0 wildcard mask indicates an exact match for the 3.3.3.9 address\\n\\nNote: While the implementation differs slightly (Juniper uses interface-level passive configuration while Huawei uses network statements), the functional result is equivalent - the loopback interface\\'s address will be advertised in OSPF without forming adjacencies on that interface.', 'trans_template': 'ospf 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'area 0.0.0.0', 'parent_command': ['ospf 1'], 'root': 'ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nospf 1\\n area 0.0.0.0\\n  network 3.3.3.9 0.0.0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Huawei, OSPF configuration starts with \"ospf [process-id]\". Here we use process ID 1 as it\\'s commonly the default.\\n2. The area is specified in dotted decimal format (0.0.0.0 for area 0)\\n3. Instead of marking an interface as passive, Huawei uses the \"network\" command to advertise the loopback address directly\\n4. The 0.0.0.0 wildcard mask indicates an exact match for the 3.3.3.9 address\\n\\nNote: While the implementation differs slightly (Juniper uses interface-level passive configuration while Huawei uses network statements), the functional result is equivalent - the loopback interface\\'s address will be advertised in OSPF without forming adjacencies on that interface.', 'trans_template': 'area 0.0.0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 3.3.3.9 0.0.0.0', 'parent_command': ['ospf 1'], 'root': 'ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nospf 1\\n area 0.0.0.0\\n  network 3.3.3.9 0.0.0.0\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Huawei, OSPF configuration starts with \"ospf [process-id]\". Here we use process ID 1 as it\\'s commonly the default.\\n2. The area is specified in dotted decimal format (0.0.0.0 for area 0)\\n3. Instead of marking an interface as passive, Huawei uses the \"network\" command to advertise the loopback address directly\\n4. The 0.0.0.0 wildcard mask indicates an exact match for the 3.3.3.9 address\\n\\nNote: While the implementation differs slightly (Juniper uses interface-level passive configuration while Huawei uses network statements), the functional result is equivalent - the loopback interface\\'s address will be advertised in OSPF without forming adjacencies on that interface.', 'trans_template': 'network 3.3.3.9 0.0.0.0'}]]": 1,
